At every point reached from this initial star, $Q(x)=f(x)$, and P6 ensures that this common root remains simple. On a retained line adjacent to such a point, the restriction of $Q$ and the submitted line polynomial are both degree-at-most-$87$ polynomial roots of the specialized relation $B$ and have the same simple value at the reached point. The one-variable specialization of KTZ Lemma 2.7 makes the two restrictions identical. Thus equality with $f$ propagates across each adjacent retained line. Connectivity propagates the same fixed polynomial $Q$ throughout the component.3c:T405,Set $p=147457$, $P=p^2=21743566849$, and $\Lambda=p(p+1)=21743714306$. Follow a valid low-degree deletion order until exactly $35585$ point vertices or exactly $23401$ line vertices remain. After either boundary is reached, delete arbitrary opposite-side vertices until exactly $35585$ points and $23401$ lines remain; their degrees cannot exceed the corresponding deletion thresholds. Charge every edge to its earliest deleted endpoint. If $I$ is the number of incidences between the remaining vertices, then $|E(G)|\le23401(P-35585)+35585(\Lambda-23401)+I$. For each remaining point $x$, let $r_x$ be its degree among the remaining lines. Since two distinct affine lines meet in at most one point, $\sum_x\binom{r_x}{2}\le\binom{23401}{2}=273791700$. If $I=4432061$, then $I=124\cdot35585+19521$, and convexity gives $\sum_x\binom{r_x}{2}\ge35585\binom{124}{2}+19521\cdot124=273791814>273791700$, a contradiction. Hence $I\le4432060$. Substitution yields $|E(G)|\le23401P+35585\Lambda-2(23401)(35585)+4432060=1282569620395349$.3d:T57c,Select every line of the first core independently with probability $\theta=2269269/46802000000$. Each accepted point on a fixed core line has at least $23401$ alternative core lines. Alternative-line sets belonging to distinct points of the fixed line are disjoint, so their coverage indicators are independent. Each success probability exceeds $1-\exp(-23401\theta)=1-\exp(-2269269/2000000)>\rho$, where $\rho=67846035/10^8$; the last inequality follows from the exact degree-$10$ Taylor certificate $\sum_{j=0}^{10}(2269269/2000000)^j/j!>10^8/32153965$. For a core line with $m\ge35586$ accepted points, Hoeffding's lower-tail inequality gives $\Pr[\text{covered points}\le23401]<\exp(-2(\rho m-23401)^2/m)<\exp(-31)$. At the endpoint, $2(67846035\cdot35586-10^8\cdot23401)^2-31\cdot10^{16}\cdot35586=109170584764560200>0$, and the exponent increases thereafter. Exact rational estimates give $\Lambda\exp(-31)<751/10^6$, so all core lines are covered successfully with probability greater than $999249/10^6$. For $r=1055070$, Markov's inequality gives $\Pr[|R|\le r]\ge1-\theta\Lambda/(r+1)=1091062427579/1452336263000000>751/10^6$. The two good-event probabilities sum to more than $1$, so some selected family satisfies both conclusions. Finally, an alternative affine line meets a fixed line in at most one point; covering $23402$ distinct points therefore requires at least $23402$ selected lines.3e:T4e8,The number of monomials in the weighted region is $M=\sum_{k=0}^{268}\binom{23401-87k+2}{2}=24690750075$. On each selected line, the graph-point evaluations of a restriction of degree at most $23401$ impose at most $23402$ independent homogeneous linear conditions. Thus all selected lines impose at most $1055070\cdot23402=24690748140$ conditions, leaving surplus $1935$, and a nonzero interpolant exists. A $Z$-independent interpolant would have at least $23402$ distinct affine-line factors while having total degree at most $23401$, which is impossible. Hence the interpolant depends on $Z$. Its weighted-degree bound gives $\deg_ZA\le268<147457$, so $A_Z\not\equiv0$. Choose an interpolant of minimum weighted degree among those having nonzero $Z$-derivative. If $A=F^eG$, where $e\ge2$, $F$ is irreducible, and $F\nmid G$, then $FG$ still vanishes at every selected graph point. If $(FG)_Z\equiv0$, reduction modulo $F$ gives $F\mid F_Z$. Hence $F_Z\equiv0$, and then $G_Z\equiv0$, contradicting $A_Z\not\equiv0$. Therefore $(FG)_Z\not\equiv0$, contradicting the minimality of $A$. Thus $A$ is squarefree. Finally, every selected-line restriction has degree at most $23401<147457$ and vanishes at all $147457$ graph points, so it is identically zero.3f:T5ee,View $A$ as a polynomial in $Z$ over $\mathbb F_{147457}[X,Y]$. Let $H$ be its coefficient content and write $A=HB$, with $B$ primitive in $Z$. Put $D=23401$, $h=\deg H$, $W=\operatorname{wdeg}_{(1,1,87)}B$, and $n=\deg_ZB$. Highest weighted parts multiply nontrivially, so $h+W\le D$. Every irreducible factor of $B$ has positive $Z$-degree. Since $B$ is squarefree and $n\le268<147457$, $\gcd(B,B_Z)=1$. Suppose first that $n\ge2$. The nonzero nominal-degree discriminant $\Delta_Z(B)$ is homogeneous of coefficient degree $2n-2$ and coefficient-index weight $n(n-1)$; consequently, $\deg_{X,Y}\Delta_Z(B)\le(n-1)(2W-87n)$. At most $h$ affine lines divide $H$. On every other first-core line, $A(L(t),P_L(t))\equiv0$ implies $B(L(t),P_L(t))\equiv0$. If also $B_Z(L(t),P_L(t))\equiv0$, then $P_L$ is a repeated finite root over $\mathbb F_{147457}(t)$. The nominal binary homogenization therefore has a repeated projective root even if its leading coefficient specializes to zero, so the line form divides $\Delta_Z(B)$; no leading coefficient is inverted. The number of content and derivative-degenerate lines is at most $h+(n-1)(2W-87n)\le(n-1)(2D-87n)$. This last expression increases for $2\le n\le268$ and reaches $(268-1)(2\cdot23401-87\cdot268)=6270762$. If $n=1$, primitivity excludes derivative-degenerate noncontent lines, while $h\le D-87<6270762$. On every remaining line, differentiating in $Z$ lowers weighted degree by $87$, so $B_Z(L(t),P_L(t))$ is nonzero and has degree at most $W-87\le D-87=23314$.40:T564,Set $p=147457$. For a component, let $a$ and $b$ be its point and affine-line densities, and let $u$ be its retained-edge count normalized by $p^2(p+1)$. Put $\alpha=23402/(p+1)$, $\beta=6075/p$, $c=\alpha/\beta=23402p/(6075(p+1))$, and $\gamma^2=6075/(23402p)=1/(c(p+1))$. The minimum-degree conditions imply $u\ge\alpha a$ and $u\ge\beta b$, while [P9] gives $u\le U_a(b):=ab+\sqrt{a(1-a)b(1-b)/(p+1)}$. Suppose $a<\beta$ and $ca<1/2$, and set $b_0=ca$. If $F(a):=(\beta-a)^2-\gamma^2(1-a)(1-ca)>0$, then $U_a(b_0)<\alpha a$. Since $U_a(b)$ is increasing below $b_0$ and $U_a(b)/b$ is decreasing above $b_0$, one of the two minimum-degree lower bounds fails for every $b$. Thus feasibility requires $F(a)\le0$. Let $\tau_0=40002062502265/10^{15}$. At $\tau_0$, after clearing positive denominators, the numerator of $F(\tau_0)$ is $129569315454799177169885256289211256171850>0$. The exact estimates $\beta-\tau_0>1/1000$, $c<4$, and $\gamma^2<1/500000$ ensure $a<\beta$ and $ca<1/2$ for $0\le a\le\tau_0$, and give $F'(a)=2(a-\beta)+\gamma^2(1+c-2ca)<-199/100000$ throughout that interval. Hence $F(a)\ge F(\tau_0)>0$ for every $a\le\tau_0$, contradicting feasibility. Therefore $a>\tau_0$. Finally, $40002062502265p^2=869787520\cdot10^{15}+115875241412985$. Since the remainder is positive and the component size is integral, the component contains at least $869787521$ points.41:T4dc,Put $D=23401$ and choose a point $x_0$ in the component. It lies on at least $23402=D+1$ retained lines; all corresponding labels take the common value $f(x_0)$, and $B_Z(x_0,f(x_0))\ne0$. In formal coordinates around $x_0$, solve $B(X,Y,q)=0$ recursively with constant term $f(x_0)$. At each homogeneous degree, the only division is by the nonzero scalar $B_Z(x_0,f(x_0))$, so the formal solution is unique. Let $Q$ be its total-degree-at-most-$87$ truncation. On every seed line, the formal solution equals the degree-at-most-$87$ line label by one-variable simple-root uniqueness, so $Q|_L=P_L$. The weighted-degree bound for $B$ implies that $B(X,Y,Q)$ has total degree at most $D$. It vanishes on at least $D+1$ distinct seed lines; otherwise their line forms would provide more