If the stated consistencies arise from simultaneous acceptance against a point table $f$, then every accepted point on a crossing line is an agreement point for the same polynomial $Q$. Because the crossing lines are parallel and distinct, those agreement points add without overlap.45:T60c,Choose two directions in $D$ as coordinate axes. Every function on $\mathbb{F}_{147457}^{2}$ has a unique reduced polynomial representative whose degree in each variable is at most $147456$. The degree-at-most-$87$ restrictions in the first coordinate direction imply that every coefficient of $X^i$ with $i\ge88$ vanishes at all $147457$ values of the transverse coordinate. Each such coefficient has degree below $147457$, so it is the zero polynomial. Applying the same argument to the second coordinate direction shows that the reduced representative $Q$ has bidegree at most $(87,87)$.

For each of the remaining $87$ directions, the restriction of $Q$ to any affine line has degree at most $174<147457$. It agrees at all $147457$ points of that line with a polynomial of degree at most $87$. Their difference has more roots than its degree and is therefore the zero polynomial, so every such restriction of $Q$ formally has degree at most $87$.

Write $Q=\sum_{r=0}^{174}H_r$ in homogeneous parts and descend from $r=174$ to $r=88$. The bidegree bound gives $H_r=X^{r-87}Y^{r-87}R_r$ with $\deg R_r=174-r\le86$. After the higher parts have vanished, the coefficient of parameter degree $r$ in each of the remaining $87$ directions is $H_r$ evaluated on that direction. It is zero because the corresponding restrictions have degree at most $87$. The directions are distinct and nonaxis, so $R_r$ has $87$ distinct projective roots. Since its degree is at most $86$, $R_r=0$. Thus all homogeneous parts of total degree greater than $87$ vanish.46:T5fd,Regard $B$ as a polynomial in $Z$ over the unique factorization domain $\mathbb{F}_{147457}[X,Y]$. Eisenstein's criterion at the prime $X$ applies: $X$ divides the constant coefficient $-XY$, $X^2$ does not divide it, and $X$ does not divide the leading coefficient. Thus $B$ is irreducible. It is monic in $Z$ and hence primitive.

The prime $147457$ is odd, so $B_Z=2Z$ is nonzero. Moreover, $\gcd(B,2Z)=1$, and therefore $B$ is squarefree. Under weights $(1,1,87)$, the terms $Z^2$ and $XY$ have weights $174$ and $2$, respectively, so the weighted degree is $174$.

For a nonaxis direction represented by $(1,m)$, a degree-one polynomial $P(t)=ct$ satisfies

$$B(t,mt,P(t))\equiv0$$

